          "explanation": "Men are underdiagnosed mainly because osteoporosis is labeled a women's disease and the USPSTF finds insufficient evidence to recommend routine screening in men, so it often goes unchecked -- even after a fracture. DEXA works in men. And men actually lose bone more slowly than women after 50; the problem is not speed but that nobody is looking, especially when secondary causes like steroids or low testosterone are present.",
